     "Their disobedience changes the circumstances of their lives. They become aware of their nakedness, experience shame, and hide when they hear God approaching.",
     "When God questions them, Adam blames the woman, and the woman identifies the serpent's deception. Their responses reveal how disobedience has affected their relationship with God and with one another.",
     "God then pronounces judgment. The serpent is cursed, and the woman and man receive consequences associated with their disobedience. Their lives will now include pain, struggle, and death, and they will eventually be sent out of the garden.",
     "Yet within this account of judgment, God makes a declaration concerning the serpent and the offspring of the woman.",
     "Genesis 3:15 records God's words describing hostility between the serpent and the woman, and between their respective offspring. The verse concludes with the offspring of the woman striking the serpent's head while the serpent strikes his heel.",
     "The verse is sometimes called the Protoevangelium, a term commonly used in Christian interpretation to describe an early announcement or first hint of the Gospel.",
     "The passage itself does not name Jesus or explain the full details of His future life, death, and resurrection. Its immediate context concerns God's judgment on the serpent and the continuing conflict described in the verse.",
     "However, within the broader Christian reading of Scripture, Genesis 3:15 has been understood as pointing forward to the eventual defeat of evil through Christ.",
     "The New Testament develops this theme in passages that describe Jesus' victory over sin and death and the final defeat of Satan. Romans 16:20, for example, speaks of God crushing Satan under the feet of believers, while Revelation presents the defeat of the dragon, identified as the ancient serpent.",
