     "The angel answered that the Holy Spirit would come upon her and that the power of the Highest would overshadow her. For this reason, the child to be born would be called holy, the Son of God.",
     "The explanation placed the conception outside the ordinary process of human conception. According to the Gospel account, Jesus' conception would occur through the work of the Holy Spirit.",
     "Gabriel then gave Mary a sign of God's ability to fulfil what He had announced. Elizabeth, Mary's relative, was also expecting a child despite her advanced age. The woman who had been called barren was now in her sixth month of pregnancy.",
     "The circumstances of Elizabeth's pregnancy did not make Mary's situation identical to hers. Elizabeth and Zechariah had conceived through ordinary human means, while Luke describes Jesus' conception as a work of the Holy Spirit. Nevertheless, Elizabeth's pregnancy served as evidence that God's purposes were already unfolding in a situation that had seemed impossible.",
     "Gabriel concluded with a statement about God's power: \"For with God nothing shall be impossible\" (Luke 1:37, KJV).",
     "This did not mean that human beings could demand any outcome they desired and expect God to grant it. The angel was speaking about God's ability to accomplish what He had declared He would do.",
     "Mary was being invited to trust the One who had sent the message.",
