     "Joseph could have continued with his original plan. He could have treated the dream as something he did not wish to consider. Instead, Matthew records what happened when he awoke.",
     "\"Then Joseph being raised from sleep did as the angel of the Lord had bidden him, and took unto him his wife\" (Matthew 1:24, KJV).",
     "The account is direct. Joseph heard the instruction and acted on it.",
     "His decision was significant because it meant accepting a future he had not anticipated. He would take Mary as his wife and become responsible for helping protect and care for the family into which Jesus would be born.",
     "Joseph's obedience was not merely an inward feeling. It became visible through his actions.",
     "The Bible does not tell us every conversation Joseph and Mary had after the dream. It does not describe all the questions he may have faced from relatives or members of the community. We should not invent those details.",
     "What Matthew does establish is that Joseph followed the angel's instruction rather than carrying out his earlier plan.",
     "His obedience required him to reconsider his understanding of the circumstances and accept the responsibility God had placed before him.",
     "The events that followed would continue to demand practical action. Luke's Gospel describes Joseph travelling with Mary from Nazareth to Bethlehem because of the registration ordered by Caesar Augustus. There, Jesus was born.",
     "Later, Matthew records further instructions Joseph received concerning the safety of Mary and Jesus. Joseph would be called to protect his family by taking them to Egypt and eventually returning to the land of Israel.",
     "His story therefore includes repeated decisions in response to the circumstances facing his family.",
