     "The chief priests were associated with the religious leadership of the Jewish people, while the scribes were recognised for their knowledge and interpretation of the Law and other sacred writings. Together, they were able to identify the biblical prophecy relevant to Herod's question.",
     "They answered by pointing to Bethlehem in Judea.",
     "Their response is significant because the location was not discovered through political influence or speculation. It was identified from the prophetic Scriptures. The wise men had observed a star, Herod had asked a question, and the religious leaders supplied an answer grounded in God's revealed Word.",
     "Yet the story also presents a sobering contrast. The leaders could identify the location of the Messiah's birth, but Matthew does not record them joining the wise men on their journey to worship Jesus.",
     "We should be careful not to claim that none of these leaders ever believed in Jesus or that Matthew explains every individual's private response. The account simply does not describe them travelling to Bethlehem with the wise men. It highlights a difference between knowing what Scripture says and personally responding to the truth it reveals.",
