   "9. Joseph's concern about Archelaus ruling in Judea.",
   "10. The warning that directed Joseph towards Galilee.",
   "11. The family's settlement in Nazareth.",
   "12. Matthew's statement concerning the prophets and the name Nazarene.",
   "Write in your own words and distinguish what the biblical text explicitly records from details it does not provide.",
   "Part 2 — Character Study: Joseph and Herod",
   "Compare Joseph and Herod as presented in Matthew 2.",
   "For Joseph, discuss:",
   "1. His response to the angel's warning.",
   "2. His willingness to change plans.",
   "3. His concern for the safety of Mary and Jesus.",
   "4. His response to changing circumstances.",
   "5. The qualities of responsible leadership demonstrated by his actions.",
   "For Herod, discuss:",
   "1. His response to the announcement of the newborn King.",
   "2. His use of deception.",
   "3. His desire to preserve his position.",
   "4. His treatment of innocent children.",
   "5. The dangers of power without justice and compassion.",
   "Conclude by explaining how their contrasting responses illustrate the difference between responsible leadership and the destructive pursuit of control.",
   "Part 3 — Prophecy and Biblical Connections",
   "Study the following passages:",
   "•\tHosea 11:1",
   "•\tJeremiah 31:15",
   "•\tIsaiah 11:1",
   "•\tMatthew 2:13–18",
   "•\tMatthew 2:19–23",
   "•\tLuke 2:39–40",
   "For each passage, identify its main message and explain its relevance to the topic.",
   "Your work must address:",
   "1. Hosea 11:1 in its original context and Matthew's application of the passage to Jesus.",
   "2. Jeremiah 31:15 in relation to the grief associated with the tragedy in Bethlehem.",
   "3. Isaiah 11:1 and the possible connection between the word netser and Matthew's statement about Nazareth.",
   "4. The distinction between explicit biblical quotations and broader prophetic interpretations.",
   "5. The importance of reading prophecy in context.",
   "Part 4 — Suffering, Justice, and Compassion",
   "Write an essay of 600–800 words titled:",
   "“Responding to Suffering Without Losing Hope.”",
   "Discuss:",
   "1. The suffering caused by Herod's command.",
   "2. Why the deaths of the children must be treated as a serious tragedy.",
   "3. The significance of Matthew's reference to Jeremiah's language of mourning.",
   "4. Why hope in God's purposes must never become an excuse for human cruelty.",
   "5. How Christians can support people who are grieving.",
   "6. How communities can strengthen the protection of children and vulnerable people.",
   "7. How faith can coexist with honest questions and sorrow.",
   "Support your essay with relevant biblical passages and practical examples.",
   "Part 5 — Responsible Decision-Making",
   "Create a practical guide titled “How to Make Wise Decisions When Circumstances Change.”",
   "Use Joseph's journey as your starting point.",
   "Your guide should explain how to:",
   "1. Identify the immediate problem.",
