     "The presence of the Spirit further emphasizes the divine significance of the event. The Father's declaration and the Spirit's descent appear together in the Gospel accounts, drawing attention to the relationship between the Father, the Son, and the Spirit.",
     "Christians understand this passage as an important biblical foundation for reflecting on the relationship between the persons of the Trinity. The passage itself identifies the Son, records the Spirit's descent, and reports the Father's voice.",
     "It is important, however, not to confuse Jesus' public revelation with the beginning of His existence or identity as the Son. The Gospel accounts present the baptism as a moment of revelation and the beginning of His public ministry, not as an event that transformed an ordinary man into the Son of God.",
     "The Father's words also echo the themes of the Old Testament. Psalm 2 speaks of the king as God's Son, while Isaiah 42 describes God's chosen servant in whom He delights and upon whom He places His Spirit. The baptism brings these themes together in the person of Jesus.",
