    "title": "Jesus Goes Up the Mountain to Teach",
    "scripture": "Matthew 5:1–2; Matthew 4:23–25; Luke 6:17–20",
    "narrative": [
     "Jesus' ministry was attracting growing attention. He travelled throughout Galilee, teaching in synagogues, proclaiming the good news of the Kingdom, and healing people suffering from various illnesses.",
     "News about Him spread, and people came from different places to hear Him and seek His help.",
     "Among those who followed Him were people with different backgrounds, experiences, responsibilities, and needs. Some were familiar with the Scriptures. Others were struggling with sickness, poverty, grief, or social difficulties. Many were searching for hope and a better understanding of God's purposes.",
     "Matthew tells us that when Jesus saw the crowds, He went up on a mountain. His disciples came to Him, and He began to teach them.",
     "The mountain became the setting for one of the most influential collections of teachings in the New Testament.",
     "Jesus was not simply offering advice about how to become more successful or comfortable. He was explaining the values and responsibilities of life under God's reign.",
     "His teaching challenged familiar assumptions about greatness, righteousness, happiness, and success. The people would hear that those who were humble, merciful, pure in heart, and committed to righteousness were blessed in God's sight.",
     "The Sermon on the Mount was addressed particularly to Jesus' disciples, while the wider narrative also presents crowds listening to His teaching. It was therefore both instruction for those following Him and a public declaration of the nature of His Kingdom.",
     "Jesus taught with authority. He did not merely repeat popular opinions or tell people what they wanted to hear. He called them to examine their hearts and reconsider how they lived.",
     "As the sermon unfolded, His listeners would encounter a vision of righteousness that reached beyond public reputation into the hidden motives of the human heart.",
     "The mountain was the setting. The message was the Kingdom. The challenge was transformation."
    ],
