   {
    "title": "The Ten Virgins: Readiness and Faithful Preparation",
    "scripture": "Matthew 25:1–13; Matthew 24:42–44; 1 Thessalonians 5:1–8",
    "narrative": [
     "Jesus told a parable about ten virgins who took their lamps and went out to meet a bridegroom.",
     "Five were described as wise, and five as foolish.",
     "The wise ones took oil with their lamps, while the foolish ones took their lamps without bringing additional oil. The bridegroom was delayed, and all ten became drowsy and fell asleep.",
     "At midnight, a cry announced that the bridegroom was arriving. The women woke and prepared their lamps.",
     "The foolish ones discovered that their lamps were going out. They asked the wise ones to share their oil, but the wise ones replied that there might not be enough for everyone and advised them to go and buy oil for themselves.",
     "While the foolish ones were away, the bridegroom arrived. Those who were ready went in with him to the wedding banquet, and the door was shut.",
     "Later, the other women arrived and called out, asking the bridegroom to open the door. He answered that he did not know them.",
     "Jesus concluded the parable by instructing His listeners to keep watch because they did not know the day or hour.",
     "The story appears in Jesus' teaching about readiness for His return. Its central message is that His followers must remain prepared rather than assuming that they can postpone faithfulness until the last moment.",
     "All ten women initially appeared to be participating in the same event. All had lamps, all expected the bridegroom, and all experienced the delay. Yet their preparation differed.",
     "The delay revealed a difference that was not obvious at first. When the bridegroom arrived, the women could no longer correct their lack of preparation in time to join the celebration.",
     "The parable warns against treating spiritual readiness as something that can always be postponed.",
     "People sometimes delay important responsibilities because they assume there will be another opportunity. They may plan to correct dishonest behavior later, begin learning when an examination is near, repair a damaged relationship only when it becomes urgent, or take spiritual life seriously at some unspecified future time.",
     "But responsible living requires preparation before a crisis arrives.",
     "The parable does not invite people to calculate a date for Jesus' return. It teaches the opposite: because the timing is unknown, readiness must be ongoing.",
     "The oil in the story should not be assigned an elaborate symbolic meaning without support from the passage. Jesus' stated emphasis is on being ready and watchful.",
     "Readiness is not simply appearing religious when others are watching. It involves a life of genuine faithfulness, obedience, and perseverance.",
     "The story also reminds us that some responsibilities cannot be fulfilled at the last moment by borrowing another person's preparation. We can encourage each other, teach one another, and offer support, but each person must respond personally to God.",
     "The lesson is not to live in panic. It is to live responsibly and consistently, knowing that we do not control the timing of future events."
    ],
    "lessons": [
     "Prepare Before the Moment of Need: Responsible people do not wait until a crisis arrives before taking every necessary step.",
     "Make Readiness a Daily Practice: Faithfulness should be part of ordinary life, not something reserved for emergencies.",
     "Do Not Assume You Can Always Postpone Obedience: Delay can create consequences that later opportunities may not fully correct.",
     "Recognize Personal Responsibility: Other people can support and encourage us, but they cannot make every personal decision on our behalf.",
     "Remain Watchful Without Fearful Speculation: Jesus calls His followers to faithful readiness, not to invent dates or live in constant panic."
    ],
    "reflections": [
     "What responsibilities do people commonly postpone until it is almost too late?",
     "How can you prepare spiritually and practically for responsibilities you know are coming?",
     "Why is it dangerous to assume that there will always be another opportunity to change?",
     "How can friends encourage one another to remain prepared without creating fear or shame?",
     "What is one area of your life in which greater consistency would help you become more prepared?"
    ],
    "assignment": {
     "title": null,
     "text": "1. How many women took lamps to meet the bridegroom, and how were they divided in the parable?\n2. What difference existed between the preparation of the wise and foolish women?\n3. What happened when the bridegroom's arrival was announced?\n4. What instruction did Jesus give at the conclusion of the parable?\n5. Explain how the parable teaches ongoing spiritual readiness rather than predicting a specific date for Jesus' return."
    }
   }
