     "After washing His disciples’ feet, Jesus put on His outer garments and returned to His place at the table.",
     "He then asked whether they understood what He had done.",
     "The disciples called Him Master and Lord, and Jesus affirmed that they were right to do so. He was indeed their Teacher and Lord.",
     "Yet He used this recognition to explain the lesson behind His action.",
     "If their Lord and Teacher had washed their feet, they also ought to wash one another’s feet. He had given them an example so that they would do as He had done.",
     "Jesus was not simply teaching a new procedure for cleaning feet. He was showing the disciples what their relationships should look like.",
     "They were not to use their positions as disciples to demand special treatment. Instead, they were to serve one another with humility.",
     "Jesus also declared that a servant is not greater than his master, nor is the one sent greater than the one who sent him. If the disciples had received His example, they should put it into practice.",
     "He concluded by connecting blessing with action: knowing these things was not enough; they were blessed if they did them.",
     "The distinction is important. A person may understand biblical teaching and still fail to apply it. Knowledge is valuable, but discipleship involves obedience.",
     "In Christian communities, service can take many forms. It may involve teaching someone patiently, helping a person who is struggling, preparing a gathering, visiting someone who is lonely, admitting a mistake, or completing an unnoticed responsibility.",
